finalText:
Fix verified on the board: heap is flat at 391916 bytes and `sent` keeps climbing indefinitely (seq 1200+, zero failures).

## Root cause

`link_send()` does **not** take ownership of `pkt.data` when it returns an error (documented in `link.h`: "On any error the caller keeps ownership"). In `send_with_retry()` (`main/main.c`), each retry allocated a fresh encoded copy via `encode_frame()` but never freed the copy from the failed attempt. Since the simulated radio is busy every 5th call, ~20 of the 50 frames/sec failed and leaked `FRAME_SIZE + 8` = 4104 bytes each → roughly 80 KB/s of internal RAM gone.

After a few seconds the internal heap (PSRAM is disabled) was exhausted, so `encode_frame()` started returning `NULL` and every send failed permanently. Confirmed in the baseline log: heap fell 391916 → 180260 → 129524 → 74560 → 23056 → 14088, at which point `sent` froze at 360 while `failed` kept climbing.

## Change

In `main/main.c`, free the encoded packet when `link_send()` fails, before the next attempt:

```c
esp_err_t err = link_send(&pkt);
if (err == ESP_OK) {
    return ESP_OK;
}
/* link_send only takes ownership on success; on error the caller must
 * free the encoded copy before building the next attempt. */
free(pkt.data);
ESP_LOGW(TAG, "send failed (%s), retrying", esp_err_to_name(err));
retries++;
```

I left `main/link.c`, `main/link.h`, the frame size, and the 50 Hz rate untouched. After rebuilding and flashing, heap stays constant and the firmware sends indefinitely (the intentional "busy" failures are retried without leaking).
